#! /bin/sh
patch -p1 -f -l $* < $0
exit $?

From 8c260a771ab1ed7d6cdb5f221854ee470e5790a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Tor=20Arne=20Vestb=C3=B8?= <tor.arne.vestbo@qt.io>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 16:02:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Apple: Make framework forwarding headers include path
 agnostic

In 8d4d1c58cd37939f906edcffd22c93c479d90ce9 we stopped pointing the
include paths into the framework Header directories, as doing so broke
Clang modules.

But we still needed a mechanism for users to include plain <qfoo.h>,
so we started generating forwarding headers for framework modules,
that redirected to the framework header via #include_next.

However the #include_next mechanism relies on the framework path being
available in the include path list _after_ the forwarding header. If
it's ahead of the forwarding header, the forwarded header is not found.

For qmake we always put framework paths last in the include path,
but for CMake it worked purely by accident, since CMake itself only
turns our targets into -isystem/-iframework, which will interleave
if the app depends on multiple Qt frameworks. The only reason it
worked was because clang itself parses -isystem as a separate pass
before -iframework. Which it does not do for -I/-F, so if the user
sets the CMake NO_SYSTEM_FROM_IMPORTED we'd surface a real issue.

And for pkg-config files, we generate them with a leading -F
before the -I, which again breaks include_next.

To solve this, we could try to re-order flags in the right order,
but even then we don't control the interleaving of flags that
the build system (pkg-config, CMake) might do when the app depends
on multiple Qt modules.

Instead, we make the forwarding-headers themselves agnostic to
where the framework include path lives in relation to the forwarding
header.

In the common case, where the framework comes after, we'll find
the framework header via #has_include_next, and #include_next it.
But when the framework path comes ahead of the header, we'll fail
the #has_include_next, and instead restart the lookup via a plain
include.

It would be tempting to always use a plain #include, but we also
need to account for the naked Qt `include` dir being somewhere in
the build flags. We try to put it last, via -idirafter for CMake,
but we can't guarantee it, so we need to avoid the situation that
a plain #include in the forwarding header hits the naked include
path first, before the framework, ending up with a recursive include
of the forwarding header again.

The framework_forwarding_headers CMake test has been extended to
cover the various permutations of flags, and tst_moc has been
extended to cover the new forwarding header format.

